Bio-threats in occupied senior living or high-rise buildings are distinct from general HVAC maintenance. Occupants breathe hard when stressed, but aerosolized pathogens—like SARS-CoV-2 or SARS-like coronaviruses—are easily aerosolized and drift through ventilation systems. To stay effective, they need humidification, positive pressure, and consistent airflow.

What we're seeing in public reports is often "small particles"—packets of viral RNA that linger longer than viral aerosols—that circulate in high-traffic zones. These don't stay in a closed room; they travel up stairwells, through high-touch surfaces, and enter through poorly sealed return air vents.

Current vulnerabilities include:

• Inadequate filtration: Many facilities use low-efficiency particulate air (LEPA) filters that capture particulates but lack specific viral capture;
• Poor sealing around return air vents: These are often positioned near common entry points without adequate gaskets or seals;
• Retrofit gaps: Many buildings were retrofitted decades ago before current ventilation standards were established;
• Lack of cross-contamination prevention: Some facilities rely on manual cleaning for outbreaks rather than automated systems;
• No continuous air monitoring: A few buildings have only intermittent air sampling rather than continuous real-time monitoring;
• Return air velocity limitations: Older buildings may have slower airflow patterns that prevent even effective filtration;

Strategies to shore up vulnerabilities include:

• Installing high-efficiency particulate air (HEPA) filters with high-efficiency particulate air (HEPA-H) capture.
• Sealing return air vents using rubber gaskets or metal screen covers.
• Testing return air flow rates regularly—anything below 10 cubic feet per minute is dangerous.
• Installing UV-C lights (UV-C lamps) to kill virus-carrying aerosols.
• Implementing positive-pressure ventilation systems (like positive-pressure cabinets) that force fresh air into occupied zones.
• Setting up automatic door sensors or low-friction entry methods to reduce human contact.
• Training staff to recognize and respond to suspected outbreaks.

For more specific info, you'd want to consult the CDC, the CDC's Guidance on Reducing the Spread of Infectious Diseases in Buildings, or HHS guidance on airborne pathogen recognition and control.

